If a patient has a chronic-pain and has a psychological-distress then the psychological-distress can frequently interfere with a pain-improvement for the patient and can frequently interfere with a function-improvement for the patient.
If a patient has a chronic-pain then every clinician may assess an anxiety for the patient with a validated-assessment-instrument and may assess a posttraumatic-stress-disorder for the patient with a validated-assessment-instrument and may assess a depression for the patient with a validated-assessment-instrument.
If a patient has a chronic-pain and a clinician assesses an anxiety for the patient with a validated-assessment-instrument then the validated-assessment-instrument can possibly improve an overall-pain-treatment-outcome for the patient.
If a patient has a chronic-pain and a clinician assesses a posttraumatic-stress-disorder for the patient with a validated-assessment-instrument then the validated-assessment-instrument can possibly improve an overall-pain-treatment-outcome for the patient.
If a patient has a chronic-pain and a clinician assesses a depression for the patient with a validated-assessment-instrument then the validated-assessment-instrument can possibly improve an overall-pain-treatment-outcome for the patient.
